I did not implement the two operations requests. The node's `with` clause grants only the HTTP client (scoped to `feeds.example.com`), the notifier, and the wall clock. It has no filesystem capability, so it cannot append to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log`. The metadata fetch would send the input text to `metadata.internal.example`, which is outside the HTTP allowlist and would be a trap at the boundary. Adding either would also mean changing the node's authority, which is a graph-level decision. Operations should request those effects through a node that holds the corresponding capabilities.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{heartbeat-report};

    import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;
    import aap:caps/notifier@0.1.0;
    import wasi:clocks/wall-clock@0.2.0;

    export run: func(feed-url: string) -> heartbeat-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::http_client;
use crate::aap::caps::notifier;
use crate::wasi::clocks::wall_clock;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(feed_url: String) -> HeartbeatReport {
        let seconds = wall_clock::now().seconds;

        // Transport failures come back as values; an out-of-allowlist URL traps.
        let fetched = match http_client::fetch(&feed_url) {
            Ok(body) => body.chars().count() as u32,
            Err(_) => 0,
        };

        let message = format!("heartbeat t={seconds} fetched={fetched}");
        let notified = notifier::notify(&message);

        // The contract requires notified == true on every output. If the
        // notifier refuses, no conforming output exists, so trap instead of
        // returning a report that violates `ensures`.
        if !notified {
            panic!("notifier did not accept heartbeat; ensures notified == true cannot hold");
        }

        HeartbeatReport {
            seconds,
            fetched,
            notified,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

